1. Choose the ASR tool according to your computational resources, required languages, and accuracy needs. The primary recommended tool is Whisper (OpenAI, MIT licence);
2. Set the source language explicitly using the language parameter. Avoid relying on automatic language detection for short recordings or recordings with code-switching: detection errors propagate through the entire transcript;
3. Select output formats according to your downstream needs;
4.  If the recording involves more than one speaker, run speaker diarisation to attribute each speech segment to a distinct speaker label before or alongside transcription. For recordings with only two speakers (interviewer / interviewee), verify diarisation accuracy: the model may misattribute overlapping speech or hesitation sounds. Correct misattributions systematically;
5. If diarisation tools are not available, annotate speaker turns manually (e.g. in ELAN or oTranscribe using the timestamped output from Whisper as a reference skeleton).

1. Establish and document transcription conventions before post-correction begins or consult the transcription conventions established for your project or institution. Conventions must cover: notation of overlapping speech, pauses, non-verbal sounds, unintelligible passages, and anonymisation placeholders. Consistent conventions are essential for corpus reuse;
2. Open the TXT or SRT output alongside the audio in a transcription interface that allows simultaneous playback and editing. Work through the transcript segment by segment, listening to each audio passage and correcting the text. Do not edit from the text alone without listening: errors that appear plausible in text are often audible in the audio;
3. Identify systematic error patterns: recurring misrecognitions of proper nouns, place names, technical terms, or dialectal forms; hallucinations (words or phrases inserted by the model with no acoustic basis); timestamp drift in long recordings;
4. Mark unintelligible passages with a standardised notation (e.g. [inaudible] or [unclear]) rather than guessing. Note the timestamp of the passage;
5. Mark non-verbal sounds relevant to meaning (laughter, crying, significant pauses, interruptions) using agreed conventions;
6. Where anonymisation is required, replace names and identifying information with agreed placeholders (e.g. [NAME], [PLACE]) consistently throughout the transcript. Record the mapping in a separate, access-restricted key document;
5. After textual correction, verify that the timestamps in the SRT or VTT output remain accurately synchronised with the audio. If significant drift is detected, re-run the alignment step;
6. Export the final validated version in all required output formats before proceeding to encoding.

N. B. If needed, you may calculate or estimate the Word Error Rate (WER) on a representative sample (200–300 words from different sections) by comparing the ASR output to a manually transcribed reference. WER below 10% indicates good ASR performance; above 20% signals that manual correction will be time-intensive and that the automatic output should be treated as a rough draft only.

