description:
FFmpeg is a widely used open-source multimedia framework designed for the processing, conversion, analysis, and playback of audio and video files. It supports a broad range of multimedia formats, from legacy and obscure formats to the most recent standards, and is compatible with numerous operating systems and computing environments, including Linux, macOS, Windows, BSD, and Solaris.

The FFmpeg suite includes several command-line tools:

- ffmpeg: a command-line utility used to convert multimedia files between formats, as well as to encode, decode, transcode, stream, mux, demux, and filter audio and video data;
- ffplay: a lightweight media player based on SDL and FFmpeg libraries, used for the playback of multimedia content;
- ffprobe: a multimedia stream analysis tool used to inspect and retrieve technical information about media files.

Due to its flexibility, portability, and extensive format support, FFmpeg is widely employed in research workflows involving audiovisual materials, including corpus preparation, media preservation, audio extraction, format normalisation, and spoken-language data processing.

