Export images, regions, annotations, transcriptions, metadata, evaluation results, and provenance in documented formats. Publish the elements permitted by source rights and project policy. Provide a dataset card or equivalent documentation describing composition, intended uses, exclusions, limitations, biases, licences, creators, funding, versions, and citation.

Test each export by reopening it and reconstructing a small set of records, regions, and source links. Include a README, field definitions, annotation guide, processing history, software environment, evaluation report, and instructions for reproducing or extending the workflow. Add checksums or file inventories for deposited packages when appropriate.

Assign versions and dates to datasets, annotations, models, and code. State the rights and licences for source images, derived files, annotations, code, and models separately. Create persistent identifiers where the chosen repository supports them. Link tools, code, models, datasets, publications, tutorials, and repository records to the SSH Open Marketplace workflow.

Practical checks and decisions:

- confirm that every public file is covered by an appropriate rights statement;
- remove credentials, private paths, personal data, and restricted material from the publication package;
- verify that documentation names the exact released versions;
- test a clean installation or reuse scenario when code is published;
- record citation instructions, contact information, maintenance status, and known limitations.

Tool options:

Institutional repositories, Nakala, Zenodo, Hugging Face, GitHub, GitLab, data catalogues, and the SSH Open Marketplace can carry different parts of the publication package.

Outputs:

- reusable exports;
- code and environment documentation;
- dataset and model documentation;
- persistent identifiers and citations;
- licences, rights, and access statements;
- links between the workflow and its related resources.

Writefull is a light-weight app that uses data from Google Books (5+ million books) and the Web to improve your writing, It compares small sections of your text to a large data set of writing found online and in Google Books. All you need to do is select a chunk of your text in your browser or text editing software, activate the Writefull popover, and choose one of its five options:







1) check the number of results (how often the chunk appears in Google Books or the Web);



2) compare the number of results (if your selected chunk appears more or less frequently than suggestions from online);



3) see examples in context (display several examples of how your selected chunk is used in different texts);



4) find words in context (see which words are used most often in your context, which you specify in your text by a star. For example, 'research should addingFilenamesPH.sh mergeTaporJason.sh this issue' will give you a list with 'research should address this issue', 'research should explore this issue', 'research should examine this issue', etc.);



5) find synonyms in context (see which synonyms of a word are used most often in your context, with you specifying the word you want synonyms for by two stars. For example: 'a \*difficult\* situation' will suggest 'an awkward situation', 'a serious situation', 'an embarrassing situation', etc).







The app works with a small popover which mean you can activate it from whatever writing tool you're using. No need to change windows - no interruption of your workflow.







N.B. Writefull 2.0 will be launching soon and will eliminate the need to purchase queries in the Web version. The $5 membership fee still applies.

