Transform validated results into documented fields. These may include source identifier, collection, shelfmark, document, page, region, object type, caption, represented person or object, creator roles, place, date, technique, information source, confidence, validation status, source URL, processing method, and software version.

Define a minimum field table with the field name, definition, data type, allowed values, source, validation rule, and example. Preserve document and region identifiers so that every record can be traced to the source image and processing step. Use separate fields or records for source metadata, machine predictions, manual corrections, and expert assertions.

Test the model on a small export before transforming the complete results. Check required fields, repeated values, empty values, date and identifier formats, one-to-many relationships, and links between documents, pages, regions, text, and entities. Record confidence, validation status, reviewer, and validation date for information produced during the workflow.

Practical checks and decisions:

- preserve original source values alongside normalised values when both are useful;
- use controlled values for classes and statuses;
- validate identifier uniqueness and referential integrity;
- document how missing, unknown, uncertain, and inapplicable values are represented;
- reopen the exported data and reconstruct a sample of source-to-result links.

Tool options:

OpenRefine, pandas, R, SQL databases, spreadsheets with controlled schemas, RDF tools, repository metadata forms, or platform exports are possible choices.

Outputs:

- documented data model;
- provenance-aware metadata;
- CSV, JSON, XML, RDF, or database export;
- field-level validation rules.

Select the people, places, works, organisations, object types, or concepts for which stable identifiers will improve research or reuse. Begin with a frequent and well-documented subset. Generate or search for candidate matches, then compare names, dates, roles, places, collection context, and other available evidence.

Keep the original value, candidate identifiers, accepted identifier, evidence, match type, confidence, reviewer, and validation date. Preserve unresolved and ambiguous cases for later review. Do not force a match when the source information is insufficient.

Practical checks and decisions:

- choose reference systems whose scope and persistence fit the corpus;
- test reconciliation on a small sample before batch matching;
- review high-frequency and high-impact entities manually;
- distinguish exact, probable, broader, narrower, and rejected matches;
- record the reference-system version or access date when relevant.

Tool options:

OpenRefine reconciliation, Wikidata tools, BnF Data, IdRef, VIAF, Getty vocabularies, Iconclass, or institutional authorities can be used according to the corpus.

Outputs:

- aligned entities and stable identifiers;
- reconciliation log;
- uncertainty and validation fields.

1. Choose the ASR tool according to your computational resources, required languages, and accuracy needs. The primary recommended tool is Whisper (OpenAI, MIT licence);
2. Set the source language explicitly using the language parameter. Avoid relying on automatic language detection for short recordings or recordings with code-switching: detection errors propagate through the entire transcript;
3. Select output formats according to your downstream needs;
4.  If the recording involves more than one speaker, run speaker diarisation to attribute each speech segment to a distinct speaker label before or alongside transcription. For recordings with only two speakers (interviewer / interviewee), verify diarisation accuracy: the model may misattribute overlapping speech or hesitation sounds. Correct misattributions systematically;
5. If diarisation tools are not available, annotate speaker turns manually (e.g. in ELAN or oTranscribe using the timestamped output from Whisper as a reference skeleton).

1. Establish and document transcription conventions before post-correction begins or consult the transcription conventions established for your project or institution. Conventions must cover: notation of overlapping speech, pauses, non-verbal sounds, unintelligible passages, and anonymisation placeholders. Consistent conventions are essential for corpus reuse;
2. Open the TXT or SRT output alongside the audio in a transcription interface that allows simultaneous playback and editing. Work through the transcript segment by segment, listening to each audio passage and correcting the text. Do not edit from the text alone without listening: errors that appear plausible in text are often audible in the audio;
3. Identify systematic error patterns: recurring misrecognitions of proper nouns, place names, technical terms, or dialectal forms; hallucinations (words or phrases inserted by the model with no acoustic basis); timestamp drift in long recordings;
4. Mark unintelligible passages with a standardised notation (e.g. [inaudible] or [unclear]) rather than guessing. Note the timestamp of the passage;
5. Mark non-verbal sounds relevant to meaning (laughter, crying, significant pauses, interruptions) using agreed conventions;
6. Where anonymisation is required, replace names and identifying information with agreed placeholders (e.g. [NAME], [PLACE]) consistently throughout the transcript. Record the mapping in a separate, access-restricted key document;
5. After textual correction, verify that the timestamps in the SRT or VTT output remain accurately synchronised with the audio. If significant drift is detected, re-run the alignment step;
6. Export the final validated version in all required output formats before proceeding to encoding.

N. B. If needed, you may calculate or estimate the Word Error Rate (WER) on a representative sample (200–300 words from different sections) by comparing the ASR output to a manually transcribed reference. WER below 10% indicates good ASR performance; above 20% signals that manual correction will be time-intensive and that the automatic output should be treated as a rough draft only.

Generate visual features from reliably extracted images and use them for nearest-neighbour search, clustering, motif comparison, duplicate detection, or the study of visual circulation. Define a small query set with domain experts, including expected similarities, difficult examples, and visually close images that should remain distinct.

Document cropping, background treatment, resizing, colour conversion, model, embedding version, index, distance measure, and filtering. Review the top ranked neighbours for each query and record relevance at a fixed depth, such as the first 10 or 20 results. Inspect clusters at several scales and link interpretations back to the source images and metadata.

Practical checks and decisions:

- compare results for complete pages and extracted image regions when appropriate;
- identify whether similarity follows subject, composition, technique, colour, layout, or digitisation artefacts;
- keep embedding and index versions linked to the input manifest;
- record expert judgements and useful counterexamples;
- treat similarity results as candidates for documented interpretation.

Tool options:

Distant Viewing Toolkit, OpenCLIP or SigLIP scripts, FAISS, PixPlot, Panoptic, AIKON, Roboflow embeddings or classifiers, and custom notebooks are possible implementations.

Outputs:

- image features or embeddings;
- searchable index or similarity results;
- clusters or ranked neighbours;
- expert validation and interpretation record.

The Collection Registry serves as a catalog of collections which occurred within the scope of research projects or serves as a basis for them;
links data whose data models and the description of a collection for technical reuse by services such as search or analysis tools;
serves to manage collection descriptions. These can include, additionally to digitally accessible, analog, protected or offline collections.

WHAT IS THE PURPOSE OF THE COLLECTION REGISTRY?
- To describe distributed collections in one place and to process them together in other services (e.g., Generic search, Cosmotool);
- to make collections visible in the Collection Registry which are otherwise difficult to find;
- to document own collections and make them demonstrable for other scientists;
- to be able to manage relevant collections in the sense of an internal catalog.

WHO IS THE TARGET GROUP FOR THE COLLECTION REGISTRY?
- Individual scientists
- Research projects
- Research associations
- Institutions

HOW TO USE THE COLLECTION REGISTRY?
A DARIAH account is required to use the Collection Registry. DARIAH-DE provides the Collection Registry as a central service. Local usage scenarios can also be implemented in cooperation with organizations.

La  transcription et son l'alignement avec le son ou l'image impose l'utilisation d'un outil spécialisé. Différents outils peuvent être utilisés en fonction des besoins de l'analyse linguistique qui sera réalisée avec la transcription.

Les logiciels libres et gratuits qui peuvent être utilisés sont :
- CLAN : transcription dans un format mode texte, avec son ou vidéo, et insertion possible de lignes dépendantes ;
- ELAN  : transcription dans un format partition, avec possibilité de structuration des données de mode temporel ou structurel ;
- PRAAT : transcription dans un format partition pour la phonétique ou la phonologie, avec son seulement mais aussi avec analyse avancée du son (spectrogramme, courbe F0, etc.) ;
- Transcriber : transcription dans un format mode texte, avec son seulement.

English translation

Transcription and its alignment with sound or image require the use of specialized software. Different tools can be used depending on the needs of the linguistic analysis that will be carried out with the transcription.

The following free and open-source software tools can be used:

CLAN: transcription in a text-based format, with audio or video, and the possibility of adding dependent tiers;
ELAN: transcription in a tier-based format, with the possibility of structuring data in a temporal or structural mode;
PRAAT: transcription in a tier-based format for phonetics or phonology, with audio only, as well as advanced sound analysis (spectrograms, F0 contour, etc.);
Transcriber: transcription in a text-based format, with audio only.

