Start with the smallest set of units and classes required by the research question. Define each visual region, textual zone, class, attribute, relationship, and metadata field. Common units include page, plate, image, sub-image, photograph, illustration, map, diagram, caption, signature, handwritten note, printed label, frame, ornament, and irrelevant region.

For each class, write a short definition, inclusion and exclusion rules, positive and negative examples, and instructions for border cases. Specify how to handle overlapping images, decorative elements, frames, partially visible objects, illegible text, damaged pages, repeated captions, and several captions associated with one image. Define the relationships needed to connect pages, images, text regions, transcriptions, and source records.

Test the schema by annotating 10 to 20 varied pages or images. Revise any class that different annotators interpret inconsistently or that cannot be used reliably by the selected export format. Choose an exchange format before large-scale annotation so that the data remain portable.

Practical checks and decisions:

- decide whether regions use rectangles, polygons, masks, points, or page-level labels;
- distinguish required fields from optional enrichments;
- assign stable class names and identifiers;
- record unknown, uncertain, and non-applicable values explicitly;
- version the schema and annotation guide together.

Tool options:

Annotation schemas can be implemented in CVAT, Callico, Label Studio, Roboflow, VIA, Arkindex, or a custom interface. COCO, Pascal VOC, Web Annotation, CSV, JSON, XML, or a platform export can carry the results.

Outputs:

- annotation schema;
- data model;
- annotation guide;
- selected exchange format.

Annotate the representative sample manually or with assisted labelling followed by correction. Include the full range of simple, difficult, rare, and negative examples selected in Step 3. Annotators should record uncertain cases rather than resolving them silently. Review these cases with collection specialists and domain experts, update the guide, and correct earlier annotations when a rule changes.

When several annotators participate, ask at least two people to annotate the same small subset. Compare missing regions, boundaries, classes, attributes, and relationships. Discuss disagreements, document the decision, and adjudicate the shared subset. Freeze and version the reviewed reference sample before using it for evaluation.

Separate training, validation, and final test material by document, volume, or collection unit before extensive model tuning. Keep difficult cases in the final test set so that reported results reflect realistic use.

Practical checks and decisions:

- check every class and important document type in the reference sample;
- review annotations at both page scale and enlarged region scale;
- verify links between image regions and associated text zones;
- record annotator, reviewer, date, guide version, and validation status;
- export the sample and reopen it in another environment when portability matters.

Tool options:

CVAT, Callico, Label Studio, Roboflow Annotate, VIA, Arkindex, or custom tools can support annotation. Assisted labelling can use a pre-trained detector or segmentation model followed by manual correction.

Outputs:

- reviewed reference annotations;
- revised guide;
- list of ambiguous cases;
- quality-control record.

Use metrics suited to the intended output. Detection can use precision, recall, intersection over union, and mean average precision. Classification can use per-class precision, recall, F1, and a confusion matrix. Segmentation can use region overlap and boundary quality. OCR and HTR can use character and word error rates. Retrieval can use ranked relevance assessments. Include expert review and manual correction time.

Evaluate on the reserved test material with the same rules used for the reference sample. When the test set is manageable, review every item. For a larger set, use a stratified sample that covers the main document types, classes, periods, institutions, and levels of image quality. Keep automatic metrics and expert assessment linked to the same item identifiers.

Measure correction effort on a realistic batch. Record the time required to accept, correct, or reject predictions, the kinds of corrections performed, and the expertise required. A method with slightly lower automatic scores may still support the project if its errors are quick and safe to correct.

Analyse errors by document type, date, source institution, visual class, layout, image quality, language, and annotation difficulty when these variables are relevant. Compare the result with the baseline and the acceptance criteria defined in Step 1.

Practical checks and decisions:

- report class-level results alongside aggregate scores;
- include representative correct results and recurring errors;
- separate errors caused by ambiguous reference annotations from method errors;
- identify groups that need additional annotation or a different procedure;
- make an explicit decision to accept, revise, restrict, or reject the method.

Tool options: 

Evaluation scripts, notebooks, FiftyOne, CVAT review and quality tools, Roboflow dataset analytics, platform reports, and structured expert review can be combined.

Outputs:

- evaluation report;
- per-class and per-source results;
- error typology;
- estimate of correction effort;
- acceptance, revision, or rejection decision.

Generate visual features from reliably extracted images and use them for nearest-neighbour search, clustering, motif comparison, duplicate detection, or the study of visual circulation. Define a small query set with domain experts, including expected similarities, difficult examples, and visually close images that should remain distinct.

Document cropping, background treatment, resizing, colour conversion, model, embedding version, index, distance measure, and filtering. Review the top ranked neighbours for each query and record relevance at a fixed depth, such as the first 10 or 20 results. Inspect clusters at several scales and link interpretations back to the source images and metadata.

Practical checks and decisions:

- compare results for complete pages and extracted image regions when appropriate;
- identify whether similarity follows subject, composition, technique, colour, layout, or digitisation artefacts;
- keep embedding and index versions linked to the input manifest;
- record expert judgements and useful counterexamples;
- treat similarity results as candidates for documented interpretation.

Tool options:

Distant Viewing Toolkit, OpenCLIP or SigLIP scripts, FAISS, PixPlot, Panoptic, AIKON, Roboflow embeddings or classifiers, and custom notebooks are possible implementations.

Outputs:

- image features or embeddings;
- searchable index or similarity results;
- clusters or ranked neighbours;
- expert validation and interpretation record.

1. Confirm that the participant's release form explicitly covers the production of a written transcript in addition to the audio or video recording. If the original consent does not mention transcription, contact the participant or their legal representative to obtain a supplementary authorisation;
2. Verify whether the transcript will be stored, shared, or published differently from the recording itself — if so, document the additional legal basis under GDPR; 
3. For pre-existing archival collections, apply the diligent-search procedure before transcribing materials without explicit release;
4. Identify any portions of the recording that must be redacted or anonymised in the transcript (names of third parties, sensitive personal data, information covered by agreed restrictions).

Extract or crop the captions, signatures, engraved text, printed labels, manuscript notes, and other textual zones required by the research question. Test a small sample before batch processing. Printed text, handwriting, engraved lettering, short labels, and mixed-language text may require different recognition models or configurations, so evaluate them separately.

Preserve the region coordinates and source image link for each transcription. Keep raw recognition output, corrected text, confidence information, model or engine version, language configuration, preprocessing, and validation status in separate fields. Review proper names, dates, shelfmarks, short strings, and low-confidence results with particular care because a small character error can affect later alignment.

Practical checks and decisions:

- compare recognition on full pages and cropped text regions when both are feasible;
- create a short manually transcribed evaluation set for each important text type;
- calculate character or word error rates where sufficient reference text exists;
- decide which results require full correction and which can remain provisional;
- retain unreadable and uncertain segments with explicit status values.

Tool options:

Tesseract, Kraken with eScriptorium, Transkribus, PaddleOCR, document-platform integrations, and custom recognition models are possible implementations.

Outputs:

- region-linked transcriptions;
- confidence and correction information;
- OCR or HTR evaluation;
- documented recognition configuration.

