Extract or crop the captions, signatures, engraved text, printed labels, manuscript notes, and other textual zones required by the research question. Test a small sample before batch processing. Printed text, handwriting, engraved lettering, short labels, and mixed-language text may require different recognition models or configurations, so evaluate them separately.

Preserve the region coordinates and source image link for each transcription. Keep raw recognition output, corrected text, confidence information, model or engine version, language configuration, preprocessing, and validation status in separate fields. Review proper names, dates, shelfmarks, short strings, and low-confidence results with particular care because a small character error can affect later alignment.

Practical checks and decisions:

- compare recognition on full pages and cropped text regions when both are feasible;
- create a short manually transcribed evaluation set for each important text type;
- calculate character or word error rates where sufficient reference text exists;
- decide which results require full correction and which can remain provisional;
- retain unreadable and uncertain segments with explicit status values.

Tool options:

Tesseract, Kraken with eScriptorium, Transkribus, PaddleOCR, document-platform integrations, and custom recognition models are possible implementations.

Outputs:

- region-linked transcriptions;
- confidence and correction information;
- OCR or HTR evaluation;
- documented recognition configuration.

