composedOf.description:
In ELAN, each category is organised as an independent tier aligned with the temporal axis of the recording.

You should create a multi-tier annotation system that reflects the different dimensions of multimodal interaction:

- Speech tier: for the transcription of spoken discourse;
- Gesture – Hands tier: for manual gestures, with a possible distinction between right and left hand;
- Gesture – Face tier: for facial movements;
- Optional Proxemics / Body tier: for shoulders, posture, and spatial orientation, depending on the recording conditions (e.g. seated or standing speaker).

The availability and relevance of each tier depend directly on the recording setup. For example, when the speaker is standing, you are usually able to capture arm movements and full-body dynamics. When the speaker is seated, as in a filmed interview, visibility is often limited to the upper body and face.

You should also keep in mind that gestural tiers do not necessarily align perfectly with the speech transcription tier. Any temporal divergence between them should not be treated as noise, but rather as analytically meaningful information about how multimodal communication is organised.

Check whether your data management plan and your documentation include:
- provenance metadata;
- technical metadata;
- intellectual property metadata;
- metadata on ethical issues;
- metadata on access and reuse (= licensing);
- structural metadata (= hierarchy);
- descriptive metadata (= contentwise).

Please add missing metadata. All information should be both human readable and machine actionable. Good file formats for human-readable content are: PDF/A, Markdown (.MD), TXT. Good file formats for machine-actionable content are JSON and XML.

