composedOf.description:
1. Confirm that the participant's release form explicitly covers the production of a written transcript in addition to the audio or video recording. If the original consent does not mention transcription, contact the participant or their legal representative to obtain a supplementary authorisation;
2. Verify whether the transcript will be stored, shared, or published differently from the recording itself — if so, document the additional legal basis under GDPR; 
3. For pre-existing archival collections, apply the diligent-search procedure before transcribing materials without explicit release;
4. Identify any portions of the recording that must be redacted or anonymised in the transcript (names of third parties, sensitive personal data, information covered by agreed restrictions).

1. Listen to a representative sample of the recording before committing to automatic transcription;
2. Evaluate: overall audio quality and signal-to-noise ratio; number of speakers and clarity of turn-taking; presence of overlapping speech, strong accents, code-switching, or domain-specific terminology; recording language(s) and script;
3. Decide whether automatic transcription is appropriate. If audio quality is very poor, or if the recording features non-standard language varieties not well covered by available models, plan for manual or semi-manual transcription instead;
4. Note the total duration of the recording to estimate processing time and post-correction workload.

A. Convert and normalise the audio file
1. Convert the recording to a format accepted by the chosen ASR tool. For example, Whisper accepts MP3, MP4, MPEG, MPGA, M4A, WAV, and WEBM, but the recommended input format is WAV (16 kHz, 16-bit, mono) for optimal recognition accuracy.
You may use FFmpeg to extract audio from video files and convert or resample recordings accordingly. Never overwrite or alter the preservation master; always work on a derivative copy and document the full conversion chain.

B. Clean the audio signal (if necessary)
1. If the sample assessment revealed significant background noise, hum, or interference, apply noise reduction before transcription. Excessive noise substantially degrades ASR accuracy;
You may use Audacity to clean the recording before transcription by selecting a noise-only segment to generate a noise profile, then applying noise reduction to the full recording with conservative settings to minimise artefacts in the speech signal;
3. If the recording has very low volume, you may normalise the amplitude using Audacity’s normalization function or an equivalent loudness normalisation filter. Save the cleaned working copy in the same format as the conversion output (WAV 16 kHz, 16-bit, mono) and document all processing operations, including software version and parameter values;
16.	Save the cleaned working copy in the same format as the conversion output (WAV 16 kHz, 16-bit, mono). Document all processing operations applied, including software version and parameter values.

1. Choose the ASR tool according to your computational resources, required languages, and accuracy needs. The primary recommended tool is Whisper (OpenAI, MIT licence);
2. Set the source language explicitly using the language parameter. Avoid relying on automatic language detection for short recordings or recordings with code-switching: detection errors propagate through the entire transcript;
3. Select output formats according to your downstream needs;
4.  If the recording involves more than one speaker, run speaker diarisation to attribute each speech segment to a distinct speaker label before or alongside transcription. For recordings with only two speakers (interviewer / interviewee), verify diarisation accuracy: the model may misattribute overlapping speech or hesitation sounds. Correct misattributions systematically;
5. If diarisation tools are not available, annotate speaker turns manually (e.g. in ELAN or oTranscribe using the timestamped output from Whisper as a reference skeleton).

1. Establish and document transcription conventions before post-correction begins or consult the transcription conventions established for your project or institution. Conventions must cover: notation of overlapping speech, pauses, non-verbal sounds, unintelligible passages, and anonymisation placeholders. Consistent conventions are essential for corpus reuse;
2. Open the TXT or SRT output alongside the audio in a transcription interface that allows simultaneous playback and editing. Work through the transcript segment by segment, listening to each audio passage and correcting the text. Do not edit from the text alone without listening: errors that appear plausible in text are often audible in the audio;
3. Identify systematic error patterns: recurring misrecognitions of proper nouns, place names, technical terms, or dialectal forms; hallucinations (words or phrases inserted by the model with no acoustic basis); timestamp drift in long recordings;
4. Mark unintelligible passages with a standardised notation (e.g. [inaudible] or [unclear]) rather than guessing. Note the timestamp of the passage;
5. Mark non-verbal sounds relevant to meaning (laughter, crying, significant pauses, interruptions) using agreed conventions;
6. Where anonymisation is required, replace names and identifying information with agreed placeholders (e.g. [NAME], [PLACE]) consistently throughout the transcript. Record the mapping in a separate, access-restricted key document;
5. After textual correction, verify that the timestamps in the SRT or VTT output remain accurately synchronised with the audio. If significant drift is detected, re-run the alignment step;
6. Export the final validated version in all required output formats before proceeding to encoding.

N. B. If needed, you may calculate or estimate the Word Error Rate (WER) on a representative sample (200–300 words from different sections) by comparing the ASR output to a manually transcribed reference. WER below 10% indicates good ASR performance; above 20% signals that manual correction will be time-intensive and that the automatic output should be treated as a rough draft only.

Provenance documentation is essential for the reusability and citability of transcripts in subsequent research. An undocumented transcript cannot be responsibly cited or reused. 

1. Create a transcription documentation record for each transcript produced. This record is distinct from the field recording sheet (which documents the oral source itself) and must cover: the ASR tool used (name, version, model size); the input audio file name and format; all preprocessing operations applied (conversion, noise reduction) with software version and parameters; the language setting and task parameter; the output formats generated; the date of transcription run; the name of the corrector and the date of post-correction; the estimated or measured WER before correction; the transcription convention schema applied; any anonymisation applied and the location of the key document; 
2. Store this record as a structured metadata file (JSON or XML) alongside the transcript files. Link it to the field recording sheet for the corresponding oral source.

Show a simple Twine structure and explain its methodological possibilities.

Suggested correspondences:
- nodes can function as concepts, hypotheses, questions or theoretical positions;
- links can function as decisions, consequences, relations or exclusions;
- loops can mark returns to unresolved problems;
- dead ends can mark limits or failed paths;
- contradictions can become productive tensions.

The key question is not only how to use Twine, but what Twine makes visible about the research process.

Expected output:
- participants understand the basic grammar of Twine;
- participants can relate Twine structures to research decisions.

Before opening Twine, ask participants to map their research question on paper.

Each participant should identify:
- 3 to 5 possible bifurcations;
- implicit assumptions;
- methodological decisions;
- consequences of following one path rather than another;
- possible contradictions, loops or dead ends.

Participants may use diagrams, lists, arrows, sketches or fragments.

Expected output:
- one paper-based map of a research question per participant.

Ask participants to translate their paper map into Twine.
The prototype should not simply tell a story. It should stage a research problem.

Suggested rules:
- each node should correspond to a decision, hypothesis, position, concept or question;
- each link should mark a relation, consequence, exclusion or shift in perspective;
- the prototype does not need to be finished;
- unresolved paths are allowed;
- contradictions are allowed;
- failure to close the structure is part of the method.

Facilitators should circulate and ask questions rather than solve the structure for participants.

Expected output:
- one small Twine prototype per participant.

After the session, facilitators may collect materials only when explicit consent has been granted.

Possible materials include:
- Twine prototypes;
- paper sketches;
- written reflections;
- anonymised discussion notes;
- facilitator notes.

No participant work should be published without explicit permission. If examples are needed for future reuse, use anonymised materials or sample prototypes created specifically for dissemination.

Expected output:

- reusable teaching documentation;
- optional anonymised research data;
- improved workflow for future workshops.

