Generate visual features from reliably extracted images and use them for nearest-neighbour search, clustering, motif comparison, duplicate detection, or the study of visual circulation. Define a small query set with domain experts, including expected similarities, difficult examples, and visually close images that should remain distinct.

Document cropping, background treatment, resizing, colour conversion, model, embedding version, index, distance measure, and filtering. Review the top ranked neighbours for each query and record relevance at a fixed depth, such as the first 10 or 20 results. Inspect clusters at several scales and link interpretations back to the source images and metadata.

Practical checks and decisions:

- compare results for complete pages and extracted image regions when appropriate;
- identify whether similarity follows subject, composition, technique, colour, layout, or digitisation artefacts;
- keep embedding and index versions linked to the input manifest;
- record expert judgements and useful counterexamples;
- treat similarity results as candidates for documented interpretation.

Tool options:

Distant Viewing Toolkit, OpenCLIP or SigLIP scripts, FAISS, PixPlot, Panoptic, AIKON, Roboflow embeddings or classifiers, and custom notebooks are possible implementations.

Outputs:

- image features or embeddings;
- searchable index or similarity results;
- clusters or ranked neighbours;
- expert validation and interpretation record.

composedOf.description:
Strategic and operational framework of the hackathon, focusing on infrastructure development through collaborative translation and multilingual ontology refinement.
In this phase, organisers establish how the hackathon contributes to:
	•	the refinement of a collaborative translation environment (e.g. MONDAECUS), assessing its workflows, usability, conceptual clarity, and limits for structured, community-driven translation;
	•	the improvement of a multilingual discovery platform ontology (e.g. GoTriple), with particular attention to how ontology terms and concept lists reflect the semantic, cultural, and disciplinary specificities of the languages represented.
At this stage, the hackathon defines:
	•	the translation scope (ontology terms, concept labels, definitions, interface elements);
	•	the languages involved and their asymmetric conceptual landscapes;
	•	the translation strategy (equivalence, adaptation, controlled divergence, commentary);
	•	the tools and workflows used for collaborative translation, review, and consolidation;
	•	the technical and conceptual limitations of both the translation tool and the ontology model (e.g. constraints imposed by controlled vocabularies, interoperability requirements, or platform architecture).
This step frames translation not as a purely linguistic task, but as a strategic intervention in infrastructure design, aiming to strengthen multilingualism, semantic precision, and inclusiveness at the level of research discovery.

Internal circulation, validation, and consolidation of all hackathon materials, while also clarifying the roles and responsibilities of participants involved in a hybrid (online and presencial) translation exercise.
At this stage, the following materials are circulated among organisers and relevant partners:
	•	the hackathon justification and framing, defining objectives, scope, and strategic relevance;
	•	the MONDAECUS user guide, tailored to the translation exercise;
	•	the moderator checklist, addressing operational readiness and support procedures;
	•	the participant survey, designed to evaluate both the platform and the exercise;
	•	the curated list of words / terms to be translated, defining the concrete scope of the task.
In parallel, this step explicitly defines and validates participant roles:
	•	Translators: participants responsible for translating the assigned terms using the collaborative platform, following the provided guidelines and documenting uncertainties or issues when necessary.
	•	Moderators: participants responsible for overseeing procedures, supporting translators, monitoring progress, and ensuring that workflows are followed consistently across online and in-person settings.
	•	Moderator-translators: participants who combine both roles, contributing to translation while also supporting coordination, clarification, and issue resolution, particularly in mixed or distributed working groups.
Given the multimedia and hybrid format of the hackathon, this step also clarifies:
	•	how coordination is handled between online and in-person participants;
	•	how communication channels are used during the exercise;
	•	how support and escalation procedures operate across both modalities.
Organisers and partners review all documentation and role definitions to:
	•	assess clarity, feasibility, and coherence;
	•	identify potential overlaps or gaps in responsibilities;
	•	propose refinements to materials, workflows, or role allocation.
All feedback is incorporated until the documentation, role distribution, and term lists reach a stable, agreed-upon form. Once crystallised, these elements are considered final and ready for execution.

Systematic review, assessment, and consolidation of the results produced during the hackathon, combining translation outputs with observational and evaluative data collected throughout the exercise.

At this stage:
	•	Translation outputs produced in the collaborative platform (e.g. MONDAECUS) are reviewed for consistency, completeness, and suitability for integration;
	•	Moderator notes and checklist results are analysed to assess workflow effectiveness, procedural clarity, and technical performance in both online and in-person settings;
	•	Participant survey responses are evaluated to gather feedback on the platform, the translation exercise, and the overall hackathon experience.

This combined assessment allows organisers to:
	•	identify strengths and weaknesses in the translation workflows and moderation model;
	•	document recurring issues, ambiguities, or platform constraints;
	•	determine which translations are ready for reuse or integration and which require further refinement.

Based on this analysis, translation results are consolidated into a stable output, and conclusions are drawn regarding:
	•	improvements to the collaborative translation tool;
	•	refinements to the multilingual ontology and term lists used by the discovery platform (e.g. GoTriple);
	•	adjustments to training, documentation, and moderation procedures for future exercises.

Where relevant, outcomes are prepared for reuse, dissemination, or documentation within research infrastructure contexts.

Before creating an archeoViz instance, ensure that you have already cleaned, structured, and prepared your spatial datasets.

This preparation includes, in particular:
- a data cleansing phase;
- alignment with shared reference frameworks and authority files (e.g. GeoNames, Pactols, PeriodO).

2.1 Data Cleansing 

Review and correct your datasets in order to ensure their internal consistency and suitability for publication and reuse.

During this subphase, you should:
- detect and correct errors or inconsistencies;
- harmonise formats, units, and coordinate values;
- remove duplicate or invalid records;
- verify the presence and completeness of mandatory fields.

This substep ensures that your spatial data are reliable and technically compatible with subsequent alignment, visualisation, and publication processes.

2.2 Data alignment with reference vocabularies and identifiers

Wherever possible, align your datasets and their contextual entities with established authority files and controlled vocabularies, including:

- GeoNames (places);
- Pactols (archaeological concepts);
- PeriodO (chronological periods);
- ORCID and VIAF (persons).

Document these alignments using rich metadata, partially compliant with international standards, in order to ensure interoperability, citability, and reuse of your data.

Load the prepared datasets into archeoViz, an open-source and freely available application dedicated to the exploration of archaeological spatial data.

3.1 ArcheoViz functionalities

Use archeoViz to explore and analyse your spatial archaeological datasets through the following functionalities:

- interactive 2D and 3D visualisations;
- basic statistical exploration;
- graphical exports (SVG, HTML);
- exports to third-party applications.

Register your archeoViz instance on the archeoViz portal to support the FAIR principles (Findable, Accessible, Interoperable, Reusable) and to increase the visibility, discoverability, and long-term reuse of your archaeological data.

This step allows you to:

- make your archaeological sites and datasets visible to a wider research community and the public;
- describe your instance using standardised metadata, aligned with shared authority files (e.g. GeoNames, VIAF, Pactols);
- ensure the citability of your ArcheoViz instance;
- enable the propagation of the record across multiple documentary and discovery systems, including BASE, HAL, Google Scholar, OpenArchaeo, and others.

