composedOf.description:
An ontology can be envisioned as a predetermined list of standardised terms or phrases to describe and categorise data. Every segmented zone can be annotated using a controlled vocabulary. It ensures that your data is:
* **Consistent**: everyone working on a document uses the same terms to describe text zones;
* **Searchable**: searching and retrieving specific information is made easier with annotated documents;
* **Interoperable**: as the annotation is standardised, data sharing is facilitated;
* **Reusable**: ontology enables you to mutualise annotated data.  

We recommend using the [SegmOnto](https://segmonto.github.io/) ontology, which is a controlled vocabulary to "describe the content of books or manuscript pages, in order to homogenise the data required by layout analysers."

A segmentation model can learn how to segment text lines and regions, and how to label them according to an ontology of line types and region types, while manually annotating the training data.  

There are usually four methods with which to perform automatic layout analysis:
* Convolutional neural networks;
* Transformers;
* Object detection models;
* Heuristics or rule-based methods.

Inside segmented zones, there are lines of text. In most cases, when you segment a text line, two elements are extracted at once:
* A **baseline** or **topline**, which defines a virtual line, passing through at least two points, on which the text is written or from which it is hanging;
* A **mask**, which is a polygon (sometimes a rectangle) defined by at least three points, and which delimits the area of pixels containing the text of the line. The mask is usually automatically calculated by the ATR system based on the baseline or topline coordinates.

Cropping a messy image is very important in order to save time later on. You can use any graphic editor to crop.  

GIMP is a free and easy to use graphic editor. For cropping and separating pages:
* Select the crop tool and left-click on one corner of the image;
* Drag the cropping area to the opposite corner in order to crop the desires area;
* Press ```Enter```

To separate pages, simply repeat this twice and save each separately.

* Hold down ```Shift``` and ```R```;
* Enter a value in degrees, depending on how you want to rotate your image;
* Press ```Enter```;
* Save.

