composedOf.description:
Write down the scientific or documentary question before selecting a technical method. Identify the unit that will be studied, such as a document, page, plate, illustration, photograph, object, caption, signature, or handwritten note. Choose one primary output for the first implementation, for example extracted illustrations linked to their source pages, segmented visual regions, transcribed captions, enriched records, or groups of visually similar images.

Formulate two or three testable questions that the resulting data should answer. Describe what a usable result looks like for the people who will review or reuse it. For example, specify whether every illustration must be found, whether precise region boundaries matter, whether captions must remain linked to images, and which errors would compromise the research.

Practical checks and decisions:

- identify the person or group responsible for scientific validation;
- distinguish the primary output from optional later enrichments;
- define the minimum acceptable quality and the errors that require manual correction;
- record the intended users, reuse scenarios, scale, timetable, and available expertise;
- confirm that the selected unit can be traced back to its source document.

Tool options:

This step can be completed with a shared document, a data management plan, an annotation specification, or a project template. No specialist computer vision platform is required.

Outputs:

- research question and scope;
- intended users and uses;
- target visual and textual units;
- success criteria and expected outputs.

Create an inventory of the collections, catalogues, local files, APIs, IIIF services, metadata exports, and existing datasets that may supply the corpus. Use one row per source or collection and record its institution, identifier system, access point, image formats and resolutions, metadata coverage, rights statement, licence, attribution requirements, and technical restrictions. Note any limits on automated retrieval, storage, redistribution, or upload to an external service.

Open a representative sample of records and images before planning acquisition. Check that identifiers are stable, image links resolve, page order is preserved, metadata fields contain the expected information, and the available resolution supports the intended task. When an API or IIIF service is involved, test a small request and record pagination, authentication, rate limits, and response formats.

Practical checks and decisions:

- inspect at least 10 to 20 records when the source is large, or the full set when it is small;
- preserve the institutional identifier and source URL for every item;
- record missing images, incomplete metadata, duplicate identifiers, and inconsistent rights statements;
- decide whether files will be downloaded, referenced remotely, or accessed through a platform;
- estimate storage, download time, and any human intervention required.

Tool options:

Institutional APIs, IIIF tools, catalogue queries, spreadsheets, OpenRefine, local scripts, and command-line download tools can all support this step.

Outputs:

- source inventory;
- rights and access table;
- identifier strategy;
- acquisition or download plan.

Build a small test set that represents the variety and difficulty of the full corpus. A practical starting point is 5 to 10 documents or 50 to 100 pages or plates. Include simple and complex examples, such as single-image pages, multi-image plates, captions, signatures, handwritten notes, short labels, damaged pages, and visually irrelevant regions. Add common cases, rare cases, negative examples, and ambiguous material so that the first test reveals the main sources of error.

Ask domain experts to identify successful cases, problematic cases, recurring ambiguities, and the scientific priorities that should guide evaluation. Record why each item was selected. If the full corpus is divided by period, collection, document type, language, image technique, or digitisation quality, represent each important group in the test set.

A published reference dataset can be used to test all or part of the pipeline before local data are prepared. Inspect its dataset card, files, labels, licence, documentation, and a sample of actual records. Compare its document types, image quality, annotation units, class distribution, and historical domain with the target corpus. Record the parts of the experiment that can be transferred and the domain differences that require further testing on local material.

Practical checks and decisions:

- keep a table linking each selected item to the feature or difficulty it represents;
- count documents, pages, regions, and classes before starting annotation;
- check that the sample includes items with no target image or text when false positives matter;
- reserve a small subset for final evaluation before adjusting rules or models;
- obtain expert approval of the sample and evaluation priorities.

Tool options:

Hugging Face datasets, institutional data portals, local samples, streaming data loaders, and simple manifests are possible approaches.

Outputs:

- test corpus or selected dataset;
- sampling rationale;
- data and label distribution summary;
- documented domain differences and limitations.

Copy or reference the selected files without modifying the institutional originals. Create a manifest with one row per source item, including the source identifier, document identifier, page or image number, file path or URL, collection, rights statement, and any field required to reconstruct the document hierarchy. Assign local identifiers only when needed and keep an explicit correspondence with the source identifiers.

Standardise file references and the metadata fields required by the workflow. Correct orientation, colour mode, file format, or resolution only when the processing method requires it. Keep the original file or remote reference, the prepared derivative, and the transformation parameters distinguishable. Detect missing files, duplicates, corrupted images, inconsistent identifiers, unexpected dimensions, broken metadata links, and incomplete page sequences.

Practical checks and decisions:

- run preparation first on a small batch and open the results visually;
- verify a sample of links between document, page, extracted image, and metadata;
- use consistent file names or manifest identifiers rather than relying on folder order;
- log each transformation, software version, parameter, date, and output location;
- keep failed items in the manifest with an explicit status and reason.

Tool options:

OpenRefine, Python or R scripts, ImageMagick, ExifTool, IIIF libraries, Distant Viewing preparation scripts, institutional pipelines, and platform import tools are possible choices.

Outputs:

- validated manifest;
- prepared files or URLs;
- source-to-output correspondence table;
- transformation log.

Start with the smallest set of units and classes required by the research question. Define each visual region, textual zone, class, attribute, relationship, and metadata field. Common units include page, plate, image, sub-image, photograph, illustration, map, diagram, caption, signature, handwritten note, printed label, frame, ornament, and irrelevant region.

For each class, write a short definition, inclusion and exclusion rules, positive and negative examples, and instructions for border cases. Specify how to handle overlapping images, decorative elements, frames, partially visible objects, illegible text, damaged pages, repeated captions, and several captions associated with one image. Define the relationships needed to connect pages, images, text regions, transcriptions, and source records.

Test the schema by annotating 10 to 20 varied pages or images. Revise any class that different annotators interpret inconsistently or that cannot be used reliably by the selected export format. Choose an exchange format before large-scale annotation so that the data remain portable.

Practical checks and decisions:

- decide whether regions use rectangles, polygons, masks, points, or page-level labels;
- distinguish required fields from optional enrichments;
- assign stable class names and identifiers;
- record unknown, uncertain, and non-applicable values explicitly;
- version the schema and annotation guide together.

Tool options:

Annotation schemas can be implemented in CVAT, Callico, Label Studio, Roboflow, VIA, Arkindex, or a custom interface. COCO, Pascal VOC, Web Annotation, CSV, JSON, XML, or a platform export can carry the results.

Outputs:

- annotation schema;
- data model;
- annotation guide;
- selected exchange format.

Annotate the representative sample manually or with assisted labelling followed by correction. Include the full range of simple, difficult, rare, and negative examples selected in Step 3. Annotators should record uncertain cases rather than resolving them silently. Review these cases with collection specialists and domain experts, update the guide, and correct earlier annotations when a rule changes.

When several annotators participate, ask at least two people to annotate the same small subset. Compare missing regions, boundaries, classes, attributes, and relationships. Discuss disagreements, document the decision, and adjudicate the shared subset. Freeze and version the reviewed reference sample before using it for evaluation.

Separate training, validation, and final test material by document, volume, or collection unit before extensive model tuning. Keep difficult cases in the final test set so that reported results reflect realistic use.

Practical checks and decisions:

- check every class and important document type in the reference sample;
- review annotations at both page scale and enlarged region scale;
- verify links between image regions and associated text zones;
- record annotator, reviewer, date, guide version, and validation status;
- export the sample and reopen it in another environment when portability matters.

Tool options:

CVAT, Callico, Label Studio, Roboflow Annotate, VIA, Arkindex, or custom tools can support annotation. Assisted labelling can use a pre-trained detector or segmentation model followed by manual correction.

Outputs:

- reviewed reference annotations;
- revised guide;
- list of ambiguous cases;
- quality-control record.

Run the simplest plausible method on the reviewed reference sample before investing in training or complex integration. A baseline can use page geometry, colour or contrast rules, OCR blocks, a pre-trained detector, image embeddings, or a small classifier. Its purpose is to measure the difficulty of the task, expose data problems, and provide a result against which later changes can be compared.

Save the predictions without overwriting the reference annotations. Review representative false positives, false negatives, poorly delimited regions, and errors on rare classes. Record whether failures come from the source images, the annotation scheme, preprocessing, the method, or a mismatch between the dataset and the research question.

Practical checks and decisions:

- run the baseline on a fixed input version and evaluation split;
- preserve code or service version, parameters, random seed when relevant, date, and hardware information;
- report results for each important class or document group;
- select a small set of recurring errors for later comparison;
- decide whether rules, model adaptation, new annotations, or a revised scope provide the next useful step.

Tool options: 

Local scripts, Distant Viewing Toolkit functions, scikit-image, OpenCV, pre-trained YOLO or Detectron2 models, Roboflow models, Arkindex processes, and document-layout libraries are possible implementations.

Outputs:

- baseline predictions;
- execution record;
- first error analysis;
- decision about further training or adaptation.

Choose a method according to the target units, available reference data, expected scale, compute resources, software maintenance capacity, and deployment context. Begin with a short pilot run on the prepared sample. Confirm that inputs, labels, outputs, and evaluation scripts work together before launching a longer training or configuration process.

Split training, validation, and test data by document, volume, photographic series, or source unit. Closely related pages or recto-verso images should remain in the same split. This reduces leakage and gives a more realistic estimate of performance on unseen documents. Record the number of items and annotations in each split and check class imbalance.

Document preprocessing, augmentation, hyperparameters, prompts or rules, initial weights, model checkpoints, stopping criteria, software versions, licences, and compute environment. Save the configuration used for every result that will be compared or reported.

Practical checks and decisions:

- compare the pilot result with the baseline before scaling up;
- inspect learning curves or intermediate outputs for obvious failure;
- preserve the best checkpoint and the final checkpoint when they differ;
- record any manual selection of examples or thresholds;
- verify that the chosen licence and hosting conditions permit the intended reuse.

Tool options:

Hosted training services such as Roboflow, platform workflows, YOLO implementations, Detectron2, MMDetection, PyTorch or TensorFlow code, notebooks, and institutional compute environments are possible choices. Projects based on rules or embeddings can configure and validate those methods at this step.

Outputs:

- trained or configured method;
- documented splits and parameters;
- model or code version;
- validation results.

Use metrics suited to the intended output. Detection can use precision, recall, intersection over union, and mean average precision. Classification can use per-class precision, recall, F1, and a confusion matrix. Segmentation can use region overlap and boundary quality. OCR and HTR can use character and word error rates. Retrieval can use ranked relevance assessments. Include expert review and manual correction time.

Evaluate on the reserved test material with the same rules used for the reference sample. When the test set is manageable, review every item. For a larger set, use a stratified sample that covers the main document types, classes, periods, institutions, and levels of image quality. Keep automatic metrics and expert assessment linked to the same item identifiers.

Measure correction effort on a realistic batch. Record the time required to accept, correct, or reject predictions, the kinds of corrections performed, and the expertise required. A method with slightly lower automatic scores may still support the project if its errors are quick and safe to correct.

Analyse errors by document type, date, source institution, visual class, layout, image quality, language, and annotation difficulty when these variables are relevant. Compare the result with the baseline and the acceptance criteria defined in Step 1.

Practical checks and decisions:

- report class-level results alongside aggregate scores;
- include representative correct results and recurring errors;
- separate errors caused by ambiguous reference annotations from method errors;
- identify groups that need additional annotation or a different procedure;
- make an explicit decision to accept, revise, restrict, or reject the method.

Tool options: 

Evaluation scripts, notebooks, FiftyOne, CVAT review and quality tools, Roboflow dataset analytics, platform reports, and structured expert review can be combined.

Outputs:

- evaluation report;
- per-class and per-source results;
- error typology;
- estimate of correction effort;
- acceptance, revision, or rejection decision.

Extract or crop the captions, signatures, engraved text, printed labels, manuscript notes, and other textual zones required by the research question. Test a small sample before batch processing. Printed text, handwriting, engraved lettering, short labels, and mixed-language text may require different recognition models or configurations, so evaluate them separately.

Preserve the region coordinates and source image link for each transcription. Keep raw recognition output, corrected text, confidence information, model or engine version, language configuration, preprocessing, and validation status in separate fields. Review proper names, dates, shelfmarks, short strings, and low-confidence results with particular care because a small character error can affect later alignment.

Practical checks and decisions:

- compare recognition on full pages and cropped text regions when both are feasible;
- create a short manually transcribed evaluation set for each important text type;
- calculate character or word error rates where sufficient reference text exists;
- decide which results require full correction and which can remain provisional;
- retain unreadable and uncertain segments with explicit status values.

Tool options:

Tesseract, Kraken with eScriptorium, Transkribus, PaddleOCR, document-platform integrations, and custom recognition models are possible implementations.

Outputs:

- region-linked transcriptions;
- confidence and correction information;
- OCR or HTR evaluation;
- documented recognition configuration.

Transform validated results into documented fields. These may include source identifier, collection, shelfmark, document, page, region, object type, caption, represented person or object, creator roles, place, date, technique, information source, confidence, validation status, source URL, processing method, and software version.

Define a minimum field table with the field name, definition, data type, allowed values, source, validation rule, and example. Preserve document and region identifiers so that every record can be traced to the source image and processing step. Use separate fields or records for source metadata, machine predictions, manual corrections, and expert assertions.

Test the model on a small export before transforming the complete results. Check required fields, repeated values, empty values, date and identifier formats, one-to-many relationships, and links between documents, pages, regions, text, and entities. Record confidence, validation status, reviewer, and validation date for information produced during the workflow.

Practical checks and decisions:

- preserve original source values alongside normalised values when both are useful;
- use controlled values for classes and statuses;
- validate identifier uniqueness and referential integrity;
- document how missing, unknown, uncertain, and inapplicable values are represented;
- reopen the exported data and reconstruct a sample of source-to-result links.

Tool options:

OpenRefine, pandas, R, SQL databases, spreadsheets with controlled schemas, RDF tools, repository metadata forms, or platform exports are possible choices.

Outputs:

- documented data model;
- provenance-aware metadata;
- CSV, JSON, XML, RDF, or database export;
- field-level validation rules.

Select the people, places, works, organisations, object types, or concepts for which stable identifiers will improve research or reuse. Begin with a frequent and well-documented subset. Generate or search for candidate matches, then compare names, dates, roles, places, collection context, and other available evidence.

Keep the original value, candidate identifiers, accepted identifier, evidence, match type, confidence, reviewer, and validation date. Preserve unresolved and ambiguous cases for later review. Do not force a match when the source information is insufficient.

Practical checks and decisions:

- choose reference systems whose scope and persistence fit the corpus;
- test reconciliation on a small sample before batch matching;
- review high-frequency and high-impact entities manually;
- distinguish exact, probable, broader, narrower, and rejected matches;
- record the reference-system version or access date when relevant.

Tool options:

OpenRefine reconciliation, Wikidata tools, BnF Data, IdRef, VIAF, Getty vocabularies, Iconclass, or institutional authorities can be used according to the corpus.

Outputs:

- aligned entities and stable identifiers;
- reconciliation log;
- uncertainty and validation fields.

Generate visual features from reliably extracted images and use them for nearest-neighbour search, clustering, motif comparison, duplicate detection, or the study of visual circulation. Define a small query set with domain experts, including expected similarities, difficult examples, and visually close images that should remain distinct.

Document cropping, background treatment, resizing, colour conversion, model, embedding version, index, distance measure, and filtering. Review the top ranked neighbours for each query and record relevance at a fixed depth, such as the first 10 or 20 results. Inspect clusters at several scales and link interpretations back to the source images and metadata.

Practical checks and decisions:

- compare results for complete pages and extracted image regions when appropriate;
- identify whether similarity follows subject, composition, technique, colour, layout, or digitisation artefacts;
- keep embedding and index versions linked to the input manifest;
- record expert judgements and useful counterexamples;
- treat similarity results as candidates for documented interpretation.

Tool options:

Distant Viewing Toolkit, OpenCLIP or SigLIP scripts, FAISS, PixPlot, Panoptic, AIKON, Roboflow embeddings or classifiers, and custom notebooks are possible implementations.

Outputs:

- image features or embeddings;
- searchable index or similarity results;
- clusters or ranked neighbours;
- expert validation and interpretation record.

Run the validated procedure on a controlled batch before processing the full corpus. Use this batch to estimate processing time, storage, compute, API use, correction effort, and failure rate. Check that identifiers, logs, intermediate results, and exports remain complete at the larger scale.

Process the corpus in traceable batches and monitor failures, hardware use, rate limits, and changes in source quality or document type. Review a sample from every batch, including low-confidence cases and items from each important source group. Pause or redirect processing when the error pattern differs substantially from the evaluated sample.

Practical checks and decisions:

- assign each batch a stable identifier and record its input and configuration versions;
- automate retries only for understood and recoverable failures;
- preserve failed item identifiers and reasons;
- keep intermediate results required for audit, correction, or reproduction;
- compare batch-level class distributions and quality indicators to detect drift.

Tool options:

An integrated platform, hosted workflow, local scripts, notebooks, cluster jobs, or institutional services can orchestrate this step.

Outputs:

- full-corpus results;
- execution and failure logs;
- reviewed samples;
- corrected or rerun batches.

Export images, regions, annotations, transcriptions, metadata, evaluation results, and provenance in documented formats. Publish the elements permitted by source rights and project policy. Provide a dataset card or equivalent documentation describing composition, intended uses, exclusions, limitations, biases, licences, creators, funding, versions, and citation.

Test each export by reopening it and reconstructing a small set of records, regions, and source links. Include a README, field definitions, annotation guide, processing history, software environment, evaluation report, and instructions for reproducing or extending the workflow. Add checksums or file inventories for deposited packages when appropriate.

Assign versions and dates to datasets, annotations, models, and code. State the rights and licences for source images, derived files, annotations, code, and models separately. Create persistent identifiers where the chosen repository supports them. Link tools, code, models, datasets, publications, tutorials, and repository records to the SSH Open Marketplace workflow.

Practical checks and decisions:

- confirm that every public file is covered by an appropriate rights statement;
- remove credentials, private paths, personal data, and restricted material from the publication package;
- verify that documentation names the exact released versions;
- test a clean installation or reuse scenario when code is published;
- record citation instructions, contact information, maintenance status, and known limitations.

Tool options:

Institutional repositories, Nakala, Zenodo, Hugging Face, GitHub, GitLab, data catalogues, and the SSH Open Marketplace can carry different parts of the publication package.

Outputs:

- reusable exports;
- code and environment documentation;
- dataset and model documentation;
- persistent identifiers and citations;
- licences, rights, and access statements;
- links between the workflow and its related resources.

relatedItems.description:
NAKALA is a Huma-Num interoperable and secure service which enables research teams to deposit all types of documented digital data (text files, sounds, images, videos, 3D objects, etc.) in a secure repository in order to share them.
NAKALA is an interoperable and secure service for depositing all types of data (e.g. text files, audio, video, images or other types) in order to share them. This repository mainly provides these services:
- assigning a PID (Persistent IDentifier) making data and metadata citable;
- permanent data access;
- dissemination of metadata through a Triple Store and OAI-PMH;
- dedicated search engine;
- customized presentation with NAKALA Press.

composedOf.description:
Avant de proposer un nouveau concept candidat, vérifiez qu’il n’est pas déjà présent dans le thésaurus en effectuant une recherche sur le site (https://pactols.frantiq.fr) et en testant, le cas échéant, différentes variantes terminologiques. Si la recherche ne donne aucun résultat, la raison peut être double : 

- le concept et ses représentations terminologiques ne correspondent pas au périmètre thématique du thésaurus (se référer à la présentation du thésaurus et au contenu de ses thématiques) ;
- il y a une lacune réelle dans le vocabulaire qui peut/doit être comblée.

After the steps above have stored their results in the repository as distributions of the original files, the data are indexed together with the other metadata and offered through **discovery services**, tailored to the specific outputs:

* page types as a search facet (**step 3**);
* OCR quality, to filter unreliable text (**step 5**);
* lemmatised full-text search and entity facets, and a viewer that highlights tokens and entities on the page images (**step 7**);
* vocabulary keywords as facets and links to vocabulary concepts (**step 8**);
* English metadata and search (**step 10**).

The distributions and their paradata are also delivered through the repository's machine-readable interfaces, with the same access rules as the original files.

In the demonstrator, this is implemented in the [AMCR Digital Archive](https://digiarchiv.aiscr.cz/), which also provides machine-readable data through an [OAI-PMH API](https://api.aiscr.cz/). Outputs from the API can be **mapped to standard ontologies** (here [AO-Cat](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7818374), based on CIDOC-CRM) and **aggregated** by other services, such as the [ARIADNE Portal](https://portal.ariadne-infrastructure.eu/). The per-document records can also be packaged as RO-Crates for reuse outside the repository.

The readiness level refers to the indexing and presentation of the enrichments. The OAI-PMH API and the ARIADNE aggregation of the repository's metadata are in operation.

Being a depositor grants certain editing rights. Data entry tools are available: the “+” (add) and “x” (delete) icons allow users to respectively add or remove roles, performances, equipment, and so on. The pencil icon allows users to edit and complete input fields.

The information related to a version is organised into different fields within the Antony application:

● credits or list of roles;
● performances (dates and locations of public performances) associated with the version;
● list of equipment, hardware, and software required to perform the work;
● documentation;
● files.

French:

6. Renseigner la version déposée

Être déposant confère certains droits d’écriture. Des outils de saisie sont disponibles : le « + » (ajouter) et le « x » (supprimer) permettent respectivement d’ajouter ou de supprimer des rôles, des représentations, des équipements, etc. L’icône en forme de crayon permet de modifier et de renseigner les champs de saisie.

Les informations relatives à la version sont organisées en différents champs dans l’application Antony :

● le générique ou liste des rôles ;
● les représentations (dates et lieux des représentations publiques) associées à la version ;
● la liste des équipements, matériels et logiciels nécessaires à l’exécution de l’œuvre ;
● la documentation ;
● les fichiers.

__Export the extracted text and metadata into a JSON file.__ The output JSON file follows the following format

```
{
	"meta": {
		(any metadata about the source document)
	},
	"text": (full text contents of the document),
	"sections": [
		{ 
			"start": (number),	<-- character position
			"end": (number),	<-- character position
			"type": (string) 	<-- e.g. "title", "subtitle", "abstract", "conclusions", "references" etc.
		}
	]

}
```

La  transcription et son l'alignement avec le son ou l'image impose l'utilisation d'un outil spécialisé. Différents outils peuvent être utilisés en fonction des besoins de l'analyse linguistique qui sera réalisée avec la transcription.

Les logiciels libres et gratuits qui peuvent être utilisés sont :
- CLAN : transcription dans un format mode texte, avec son ou vidéo, et insertion possible de lignes dépendantes ;
- ELAN  : transcription dans un format partition, avec possibilité de structuration des données de mode temporel ou structurel ;
- PRAAT : transcription dans un format partition pour la phonétique ou la phonologie, avec son seulement mais aussi avec analyse avancée du son (spectrogramme, courbe F0, etc.) ;
- Transcriber : transcription dans un format mode texte, avec son seulement.

English translation

Transcription and its alignment with sound or image require the use of specialized software. Different tools can be used depending on the needs of the linguistic analysis that will be carried out with the transcription.

The following free and open-source software tools can be used:

CLAN: transcription in a text-based format, with audio or video, and the possibility of adding dependent tiers;
ELAN: transcription in a tier-based format, with the possibility of structuring data in a temporal or structural mode;
PRAAT: transcription in a tier-based format for phonetics or phonology, with audio only, as well as advanced sound analysis (spectrograms, F0 contour, etc.);
Transcriber: transcription in a text-based format, with audio only.

